The gate matrix
01-accounts-and-security
Each row is a registered account, each column one of the per-account gates. ON, off, a dash (the gate does not apply to a group or other account: it is a route for a person) or plan (not built yet). A star after a cell is a change you have queued; the cell shows the state it would have. Nothing is on as delivered except the right to read the shared account.
Arrows move over the cells and the box below says what the gate is: its two words, what ON allows, what OFF still allows, when a change reaches the person and whether it reaches beyond SD. Enter queues the opposite state for the cell (again takes it back). R runs the queued changes of the account on the cursor's row as ONE modify.account command, shown with its effect, answered Run or Cancel; U drops them; T queues OFF for every gate that is off as delivered and on now. A (all on) queues every gate of the cursor's account that can change ON, and N (all off) every one off; they only queue, so you can turn some back with Enter before R.
Turning ON a gate that reaches beyond SD asks you to type the account's name. ssh and api are one setting in the verb, so the page sends the word that gives the pair you queued. files-write needs files and files-admin needs files-write; the page and the verb both refuse a queue that breaks that. A change does not reach a session that is already running. F changes which accounts are shown, and / goes to the next account on show whose name holds a text you type.
The home column is where a session of the account starts: ON the home screen, off the command prompt (start-menu and start-terminal). An account given neither follows the system's default, the installer's answer, which set.start.default changes. A and N leave it alone.
